<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamanrasset</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamanrasset is the main town of the Ahaggar region in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. It grew from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> settlement and a French military post of the early 20th century, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-foucauld">Charles de Foucauld</a> lived from 1905. It is now a provincial capital and a stop on the trans-Saharan route.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamanrasset.jpg" alt="Tamanrasset" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Habib kaki · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cour_d%27assises_de_Tamanrasset_%D9%85%D8%AC%D9%84%D8%B3_%D9%82%D8%B6%D8%A7%D8%A1_%D8%AA%D9%85%D9%86%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tamanrasset, also known as Tamanghasset or Tamenghest, stands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains">Ahaggar Mountains</a> at an altitude of 1,320 metres. It is an oasis, where citrus fruits, apricots, dates, almonds, cereals, corn and figs are grown despite the difficult climate. The climate is a hot desert one with very little rain, although the altitude moderates the summer heat. The name is said to derive from Imanrassaten, a noble tribe now associated with the Ajjers, which reportedly once lived in the wadi region or used it to mark the boundaries of its grazing lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The place originated as the centre of a network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> that ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>, Gao, Agades and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zinder">Zinder</a>. Under French rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> the Catholic priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-foucauld">Charles de Foucauld</a> built his hermitage here in 1905, and the fort was established by 1915. Foucauld was shot to death outside his compound on 1 December 1916 by Sermi ag Thora, acting under the command of El Madani ag <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soba">Soba</a>.</p><p>As a military post the settlement was named Fort Laperrine, after General François-Henry Laperrine, who died in the desert nearby in 1922. On 1 May 1962, near Ecker, 150 km north of Tamanrasset, a French underground nuclear test was accidentally vented. The shaft had been improperly sealed, a flame burst through the concrete cap, and radioactive gases and dust rose in a plume up to 2,600 metres high. Radiation was detected hundreds of kilometres away. About a hundred French soldiers and officials, including two ministers, were irradiated; the number of Algerians contaminated is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tamanrasset is the capital of Tamanrasset Province and the chief city of the Algerian Tuareg. Its population was 92,635 at the 2008 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, up from 72,741 in 1998. It is a centre of trade across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and into neighbouring countries, and it is served by Tamanrasset Airport and the Trans-Sahara Highway. The Museum of the Hoggar holds exhibits on Tuareg life and culture, and the commune includes the locality of Assekrem among its fourteen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tamanrasset” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After their defeat by French forces at Tit in 1902, the amenokal Moussa ag Amastan accepted French rule and befriended Charles de Foucauld at <b>Tamanrasset</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kel-ahaggar">Kel Ahaggar</a></li></ul></section>
